{%- doc -%}
  Renders a full-width ocean-wave edge treatment, absolutely positioned at the
  top or bottom edge of its (already position: relative) parent. The wave's
  fill reuses the theme's own --color-background variable for the given color
  scheme, so it always matches whatever section it's revealing — no hardcoded
  colors to keep in sync.

  @param {string} color_scheme - Scheme id whose background the wave itself is filled with, e.g. 'scheme-4'
  @param {string} [position] - 'top' or 'bottom'. Default 'bottom'.
  @param {number} [height] - Wave box height in px (desktop). Default 100.
  @param {string} [reveal_color_scheme] - Scheme id for the flat color the wave dips into. Optional —
    omit when the wave sits over an image (e.g. a hero photo) that should show through instead.

  @example
  {% render 'wave-divider', color_scheme: 'scheme-4', position: 'top', reveal_color_scheme: 'scheme-1' %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign wave_position = position | default: 'bottom'
  assign wave_height = height | default: 100
-%}

<div
  class="wave-divider wave-divider--{{ wave_position }}"
  style="--wave-height: {{ wave_height }}px;"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  {%- if reveal_color_scheme != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Guarantees the area above the crest is a clean, known color rather than
      whatever happens to be layered behind the wave in the real page (which
      can look muddy if another element's background or shadow bleeds through).
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="wave-divider__reveal color-{{ reveal_color_scheme }}"></div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The theme's own global rule is `body, .color-scheme-1, ..., .color-scheme-6
    { background-color: var(--color-background); }` — it applies to ANY element
    carrying a color-N class, not just section wrappers. Putting that class
    directly on the <svg> (to source --color-background for fill/stroke) would
    also paint an opaque background over the whole SVG canvas, hiding the
    transparent gaps at the wave's peaks. This wrapper carries the class
    instead, with display:contents so it has no box of its own — the CSS
    variable still inherits down to the SVG, but the background rule never
    matches the SVG element itself.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span class="color-{{ color_scheme }}" style="display: contents;">
  <svg
    class="wave-divider__svg"
    viewBox="0 0 1440 100"
    preserveAspectRatio="none"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      Peaks and troughs are real anchor points at y=0 and y=100, not control
      points the curve merely gets pulled toward. A control point only
      influences the curve's shape — it's never guaranteed to be touched — so
      approximating a peak that way (as an earlier version of this path did)
      left a flat, un-reached band of solid color sitting above the visible
      curve everywhere across the width. Anchoring directly at the extremes
      removes that band: the crest genuinely reaches full "reveal" color and
      the trough genuinely reaches full "wave" color.

      Two paths, same curve, different jobs. The fill path adds a straight
      line down each side and across the bottom (L1440,100 L0,100 Z) purely
      to close the shape so it can be filled — those synthetic edges were
      never meant to be seen, but stroking this single path drew them too,
      showing up as a straight line along the trough/bottom boundary. The
      line-only path repeats just the curve itself with no closing edges, so
      the stroke only ever traces the actual wave.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <path
      class="wave-divider__fill"
      d="M0,0 C120,0 120,100 240,100 C360,100 360,0 480,0 C600,0 600,100 720,100 C840,100 840,0 960,0 C1080,0 1080,100 1200,100 C1320,100 1320,0 1440,0 L1440,100 L0,100 Z"
    />
    <path
      class="wave-divider__line"
      d="M0,0 C120,0 120,100 240,100 C360,100 360,0 480,0 C600,0 600,100 720,100 C840,100 840,0 960,0 C1080,0 1080,100 1200,100 C1320,100 1320,0 1440,0"
    />
  </svg>
  </span>
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  .wave-divider {
    /*
      `.section` is a 3-col grid (margin | content | margin) and auto-places
      direct children into the centre column. Overriding that here makes the
      wave span the full section width edge-to-edge, not just the content
      column.
    */
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: var(--wave-height, 100px);
    line-height: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    pointer-events: none;
    /*
      Deliberately NOT overflow:hidden. Some themes leave a gap of a few tens
      of pixels between a section's outer wrapper (which its own flat
      background fills) and the inner box this component is anchored to. If
      the reveal cap were clipped to exactly this box, that gap shows through
      as an uncovered sliver of the wave's own color — a thin flat line sitting
      above the curve. The reveal cap below is intentionally taller than this
      box and needs to be free to extend past it.
    */
  }

  /*
    Both placements use the exact same unflipped path — three crests rising
    toward y=0, solid fill down to y=100. The only difference is which edge of
    the host they're anchored to and whose color they carry:

    --bottom: anchored to the host's own bottom edge (bottom: -1px), so the
    wave sits INSIDE the host, poking up into it. Colored with the NEXT
    section's background — used e.g. at the bottom of the hero, revealing the
    section below cresting up into it.

    --top: anchored via bottom: 100%, which places the div OUTSIDE the host,
    flush against its top edge, extending upward into whatever precedes it.
    Colored with the HOST's own background — used e.g. at the top of the
    footer, so the footer's own color appears to rise up into the section
    above it.
  */
  .wave-divider--bottom {
    bottom: -1px;
  }

  .wave-divider--top {
    bottom: 100%;
  }

  /*
    Extends a little past its own box on the side facing away from the curve
    — not just inset:0 — as a safety margin against the wrapper/inner-box gap
    described above (measured at ~12px in this theme's footer). Kept modest
    on purpose: this cap sits in front of whatever precedes the section, and
    if that's real content (product images, text) rather than open flat
    background, an oversized margin would start covering it up. 48px gives a
    4x buffer over the measured gap without reaching into unrelated content.
  */
  .wave-divider__reveal {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    background: var(--color-background);
  }

  .wave-divider--top .wave-divider__reveal {
    top: -48px;
    bottom: 0;
  }

  .wave-divider--bottom .wave-divider__reveal {
    top: 0;
    bottom: -48px;
  }

  .wave-divider__svg {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .wave-divider__fill {
    fill: var(--color-background);
    stroke: none;
  }

  .wave-divider__line {
    fill: none;
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-background) 78%, black 22%);
    stroke-width: 3;
    /* Keeps the stroke a consistent weight despite preserveAspectRatio="none" stretching the two axes unevenly. */
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .wave-divider {
      height: calc(var(--wave-height, 100px) * 0.55);
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
